// Cross-target fixture: timing a block, sleeping, and standard error.
//
// The elapsed value itself cannot be asserted -- it differs every run -- so
// what is checked is the shape every backend has to agree on: the block runs
// exactly once, its value is discarded, the answer is milliseconds, and a
// sleep of N is at least N. The lines printed inside the block are the proof
// that it ran, and in the right order relative to everything else.

println("before")
val slept = stopwatch( => {
  sleep(150)
  println("inside")
})
println("after")
println(slept > 100)
println(slept < 60000)
assertResult(true)(slept > 100)

// A block that does not sleep still answers with a number, and still runs.
val quick = stopwatch( => { println("quick") })
println(quick < 30000)
assertResult(true)(quick < 30000)

// Sleeping for nothing is allowed and returns immediately.
sleep(0)
println("zero slept")

// Standard error is a separate stream: this line is not part of the stdout
// the fixture is compared on, which is itself the thing being checked.
// A `thread` body runs after the program's own statements on every native
// target, so what a program prints is the same everywhere -- which is the only
// thing about it a program can observe here.
thread(() => {
  println("from the thread")
})
println("after handing over the thread")

printlnError("to standard error")
val message = "also to standard error"
printlnError(message)
println("done")
